        <p class="location">White Plains, New York</p>
        <h1>A history of Lakenridge and Westminster Ridge</h1>
        <p class="intro">The neighborhood around Todds Pond developed in stages. Maps, real-estate notices, architectural magazines, aerial photographs, and club records show what was here and how the neighborhood grew.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-wide">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1910 map in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1910-white-plains-map.jpg" width="1536" height="999" alt="1910 map of White Plains and the surrounding area" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>1910 map of White Plains and the surrounding area. The later Westminster Ridge neighborhood is near the map's eastern side. <span class="credit">Westchester County atlas.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </header>

      <section id="story" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="story-title">
        <h2 id="story-title">Summary</h2>
        <p>In 1901, a county atlas marked a large tract in this area “A. C. Todd.” By 1911, an atlas labeled a nearby body of water “RESERVOIR.” In 1917, real-estate notices described Westminster Park as a planned subdivision of 52 homes.</p>
        <p>The first plan did not build the whole neighborhood. Construction slowed during the Depression. In the late 1930s, architect-builder Lee Perry and the County Housing Corporation developed vacant lots in Westminster Ridge. Their advertisements showed several house types, a playground, and a lake.</p>
        <p>By 1939, a community lake club was buying the lake from the original subdividers. That club, and the shared water, became part of the neighborhood's long-term identity.</p>
        <div class="facts" aria-label="Key dates and figures">
          <div><strong>1901</strong><span>A. C. Todd appears on a county atlas</span></div>
          <div><strong>1917</strong><span>Westminster Park plan calls for 52 homes</span></div>
          <div><strong>1938</strong><span>41 homes were built or underway</span></div>
          <div><strong>$2</strong><span>Monthly lake-club dues described in 1939</span></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="early-title">
        <h2 id="early-title">Before the houses</h2>
        <p>White Plains became easier to reach after the railroad arrived in 1844. That change helped suburban development across the city, but it does not by itself establish when this particular tract was first developed.</p>
        <p>The first useful local clue is the 1901 Westchester County atlas. It places the name “A. C. Todd” on a substantial tract near the area later associated with the pond and the neighborhood. The atlas does not identify a house, and it does not show the boundaries of Todd's property clearly enough to settle the question of ownership.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1901 A. C. Todd map in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1901-ac-todd.jpg" width="1200" height="1120" alt="1901 county atlas detail labeled A. C. Todd" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>1901: the atlas labels the tract “A. C. Todd.” <span class="credit">Westchester County Archives.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1911 reservoir map in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1911-reservoir.jpg" width="1800" height="1250" alt="1911 county atlas showing a reservoir and a dam-like feature" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>1911: the water is labeled “RESERVOIR.” A dam-like feature is also drawn. <span class="credit">Westchester County Archives.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p class="note">These maps show that a waterbody was mapped in the area by 1911. They do not establish whether the original feature was natural, enlarged, excavated, or dammed.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="maps" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="maps-title">
        <h2 id="maps-title">Westminster Park on paper</h2>
        <p>Two 1917 notices identify Westminster Park as an active real-estate project. The January notice proposed 52 dwellings. The July notice names the Westminster Park Company, Norman W. Walker, Joseph J. Eberle, and a planned house costing $10,000.</p>
        <p>The notices document a plan and named participants. They do not prove that all 52 homes were built at that time.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the January 1917 Westminster Park notice in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1917-january-notice.jpg" width="580" height="710" alt="January 1917 real-estate notice for Westminster Park" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
