            <figcaption>July 1917: the notice names the company, developer, architect, and a $10,000 house.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="growth-title">
        <h2 id="growth-title">The neighborhood grows in stages</h2>
        <p>The 1939 account in <em>Architectural Forum</em> describes Westminster Ridge as a later development on estate and woodland land. It says the first build-out produced about 30 higher-priced homes by 1930, then slowed during the Depression. The County Housing Corporation later acquired about 80 vacant lots in a 40-acre tract and restarted construction in 1938.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-wide">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1939 Westminster Ridge plot plan in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1939-plot-plan.jpg" width="467" height="570" alt="1939 plot plan showing winding roads, homes, woods, and the lake" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>1939 plot plan: winding roads, wooded areas, houses, and the lake. <span class="credit">Architectural Forum, March 1939.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="estate-title">
        <h2 id="estate-title">20 Stafford Place: a possible estate house</h2>
        <p>The 1939 article says the old estate house was still standing when the neighborhood was revived. 20 Stafford Place is a plausible lead because public property records describe a 1907 Colonial of about 4,012 square feet on 0.74 acres. Local street-history research says Stafford Place was renamed in 1934 for Fred P. Stafford.</p>
        <p>Those facts make it a lead, not proof. A deed chain, tax card, city-directory entry, or period photograph would be needed to make the identification.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-wide">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the map showing the 20 Stafford Place research lead in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/20-stafford-map-lead.jpg" width="1800" height="1600" alt="Map showing an approximate marker near 20 Stafford Place and the later reservoir" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>The red marker is a research overlay near 20 Stafford Place. It is not a label printed on the original map.</figcaption>
        </figure>
